          "level": 1,
          "text": "Why the work-item lifecycle keeps status and stage separate, and what extending it costs",
          "anchor": "why-the-work-item-lifecycle-keeps-status-and-stage-separate-and-what-extending-it-costs",
          "githubAnchor": "why-the-work-item-lifecycle-keeps-status-and-stage-separate-and-what-extending-it-costs",
          "stableAnchor": "h-15a1292763df"
        },
        {
          "level": 2,
          "text": "Extending the domain still means auditing every consumer",
          "anchor": "extending-the-domain-still-means-auditing-every-consumer",
          "githubAnchor": "extending-the-domain-still-means-auditing-every-consumer",
          "stableAnchor": "h-ddf34079e45a"
        },
        {
          "level": 2,
          "text": "Adding a precondition is a bigger regression radius than adding a value",
          "anchor": "adding-a-precondition-is-a-bigger-regression-radius-than-adding-a-value",
          "githubAnchor": "adding-a-precondition-is-a-bigger-regression-radius-than-adding-a-value",
          "stableAnchor": "h-5d3ce6e16398"
        },
        {
          "level": 2,
          "text": "Two edges landing on the same status can still mean different things — a fix needs a positive marker, not an inference from the shared value",
